# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# wrangler local dev cache (from testing Cloudflare headers/deploy locally)
.wrangler/

# local Obsidian vault config — editor-local state, not repo content
/.obsidian/

# claude code local settings. .claude/* rather than .claude/ so the skills below
# can be re-included — git cannot re-include a path whose parent directory is
# itself excluded, so the parent must exclude its children, not itself.
.claude/*
# project workflow definitions, versioned with the code they describe
!.claude/skills/
# how to start this project's dev server — repo content, not machine state
!.claude/launch.json

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# scratch files from API probing
/live.json
/boot.json
/fix.json

# Third-party provider documentation kept locally for reference. The rule stands
# on size alone — the API-Football PDF is 7.3MB, and a git repo is a poor home for
# vendor binaries that are downloadable from the vendor. (It originally also said
# "not ours to redistribute from a public repo"; the repo is private now, which
# weakens that half of the argument but not the first.) docs/roadmap.md records
# what was actually read out of them, which is the part that needed to be durable.
/docs/*.pdf
/docs/sources/data/*.pdf

# Marker file the post-commit hook (scripts/hooks/post-commit) appends to; the wiki-ingest
# skill consumes and clears it. Local working state, not durable content.
/docs/wiki/.pending-ingest

# Working files for the promoted-clubs cold-start data drop (2026-08-05): raw
# FootyStats PDFs per player/club and a fabricated analytics CSV rejected as a
# data source (95% position archetypes, not real per-player data — see "A
# candidate data drop was rejected" in docs/roadmap.md). The verified
# premier_league_new_players.csv is tracked; everything else here is scratch
# or vendor material, not durable repo content.
/docs/Promoted Team Data/*.pdf
/docs/Promoted Team Data/premier_league_new_players_analytics.csv
/docs/Promoted Team Data/Coventry/
/docs/Promoted Team Data/Hull City/
/docs/Promoted Team Data/Ipswich/

# Sprint 31, public-repo pre-flight. These two are the genuinely
# FootyStats-derived files: 58 rows of transcribed per-player stats, each row
# naming the FootyStats PDF it came from, plus the same values as SQL. The
# redistribution half of the argument above becomes live again the moment the
# repo goes public, so they are untracked rather than published. Nothing about
# the shipped cold-start priors becomes unauditable: the data itself lives in
# `external_player_seasons` in Supabase, and
# docs/sprints/championship-priors.md documents the drop, the three-check gate
# it passed, and the fitted λ per metric. The other two files here stay tracked
# — premier_league_new_players.csv is plain roster fact (name/fpl_id/club/
# position) and match_report.json is this repo's own matching output, neither
# of which is FootyStats' to withhold.
/docs/Promoted Team Data/extracted/footystats_championship_2025_26.csv
/docs/Promoted Team Data/extracted/insert.sql

# Supabase CLI scratch (version check cache)
supabase/.temp/
